// Cloudflare Workers, serving `site/` as static assets and nothing else.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS RATHER THAN A SETTING IN A DASHBOARD. Cloudflare merged Workers and
// Pages, and the current flow has no "output directory" field: it runs `npx wrangler deploy`
// and reads the configuration from here. So the deploy settings live beside the code, show up
// in a diff, and are reviewable — which is the same argument this repository makes for its
// hooks and its CI being tracked files rather than machine state.
//
// NO `main`. A Worker with `assets` and no entry script is a pure static site: Cloudflare
// serves the directory and never runs code. Nothing in `site/` is generated, so there is no
// build command either — `index.html` is hand-written and the four PDFs under `site/fixtures/`
// are committed rather than produced at deploy time.
//
// THE DIRECTORY MATTERS. The repository root is the engine, not the page; pointing this at `.`
// would publish the entire source tree, `out/` included, to a public domain.
// ONE PUBLIC ADDRESS, NOT THREE. Cloudflare enables `workers_dev` by default, so
// `qatration.qatration.workers.dev` served this identical page — verified byte for byte, same
// md5, same 33002 bytes — beside the domain it is supposed to live at. And `preview_urls`
// defaults to whatever `workers_dev` is, which meant a per-version hostname for every deploy
// ever made: publish something by mistake, fix it, and the mistaken build stays reachable at
// its own URL. That is the same shape as a bad commit surviving in history, and it is worth
// closing for the same reason.
//
// HERE RATHER THAN THE DASHBOARD TOGGLE, and the distinction matters. The default is `true`
// and Workers Builds deploys from this file on every push, so a toggle flipped in the UI and
// not written down is a setting the next deploy can quietly restore. In the file it survives
// every deploy and shows up in a diff.
//
// The custom domain is unaffected: it is a Custom Domain on the zone, listed separately from
// the Worker URL, and nothing here declares or removes it. What is lost is a diagnostic — when
// the site is down, the `workers.dev` URL used to answer "is it the Worker or is it the
// domain". That is a once-a-year question; two live addresses for one site is a daily one.
{
  "name": "qatration",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-08-22",
  "workers_dev": false,
  "preview_urls": false,
  "assets": {
    "directory": "./site"
  }
}
